              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+identities"} simplicial identities
          %p#SimplicialSetIdea
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %center
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/SimplicialSetSchematics.jpg", :width => "800"}/
          %h2#remarks Remarks
          %h3#simplicial_sets_as_spaces_built_of_simplices Simplicial sets as spaces built of simplices
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The definition is to be understood from the point of view of
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+and+quantity"} space and quantity
                %strong simplicial set
                is a space characterized by the fact that it may be
                %em probed
                by mapping standard simplices into it: the set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_n
                assigned by a simplicial set to the standard
                = succeed "-simplex" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [n]
                is the
                %strong
                  set of
                  = succeed "-simplices" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                in this space, hence the way of mapping a standard
                = succeed "-simplex" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %li
              %p
                For
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                a simplicial set, the
                %strong face map
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi d
                        %mi i
                      %mo ≔
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi δ
                        %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi n
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo −
                          %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      d_i \coloneqq S(\delta^i): S_n \rightarrow S_{n-1}
              %p
                is dual to the unique injection
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi δ
                        %mi i
                      %mo :
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo →
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta^i : [n-1] \rightarrow [n]
                in the category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Δ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
                whose image omits the element
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \in [n]
            %li
              %p
                Similarly, the
                %strong degeneracy map
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi s
                        %mi i
                      %mo ≔
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi σ
                        %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi n
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      s_i \coloneqq S(\sigma^i) : S_n \rightarrow S_{n+1}
              %p
                is dual to the unique surjection
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi σ
                        %mi i
                      %mo :
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo →
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma^i : [n+1] \rightarrow [n]
                in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Δ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
                such that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo ∈
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \in [n]
                has two elements in its preimage.
            %li
              %p
                The maps
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi δ
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta^i
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi σ
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma^i
                satisfy certain obvious relations – the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+identities"} simplicial identities
                – dual to those spelled out at
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex+category"} simplex category
          %h3#visualisation Visualisation
          %p
            (based on
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+set"} cubical set
          %p
            The
            %strong face maps
            go from sets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_{n+1}
            of
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_{n}
            of
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n=1
            the set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_2
            of 2-simplices would be sent in three different ways by three different face maps to the set of
            = succeed "-simplices," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi b
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↗
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo ⇓
                              %mi F
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↘
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi a
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi c
                    %mo )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ↦
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi b
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↗
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi a
                    %mo )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\left(
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;          &amp; b
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \nearrow &amp; \Downarrow^F &amp; \searrow
                  \\\
                  a &amp;          &amp; \rightarrow  &amp;          &amp; c
                  }
                  \\right)
                  \\;\;
                  \\mapsto
                  \\;\;
                  \\left(
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;          &amp; b
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \nearrow
                  \\\
                  a
                  }
                  \\right)
          %p another one would send
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi b
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↗
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo ⇓
                              %mi F
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↘
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi a
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi c
                    %mo )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ↦
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi a
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi c
                    %mo )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\left(
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;          &amp; b
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \nearrow &amp; \Downarrow^F &amp; \searrow
                  \\\
                  a &amp;          &amp; \rightarrow  &amp;          &amp; c
                  }
                  \\right)
                  \\;\;
                  \\mapsto
                  \\;\;
                  \\left(
                  \\array{
                  a &amp;          &amp; \rightarrow  &amp;          &amp; c
                  }
                  \\right)
                  \\,.
          %p
            On the other hand, the
            %strong degeneracy maps
            go the other way round and send sets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_n
            of
            = succeed "-simplices" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_{n+1}
            of
            = succeed "-simplices" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
            = succeed "-simplex" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-simplex" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n=1
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi a
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi f
                          %mtd
                            %mi b
                    %mo )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ↦
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi b
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo ↗
                              %mi f
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo ⇓
                              %mi Id
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo ↘
                              %mi Id
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi a
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi f
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi b
                    %mo )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\left(
                  \\array{
                  a
                  \&amp;\stackrel{f}{\to}&amp;
                  b
                  }
                  \\right)
                  \\;\;
                  \\mapsto
                  \\;\;
                  \\left(
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;            &amp; b
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \nearrow_f &amp; \Downarrow^{Id} &amp; \searrow^{Id}
                  \\\
                  a &amp;            &amp; \stackrel{f}\to &amp;               &amp; b
                  }
                  \\right)
                  \\,.
          %p
            Notice the
            = succeed "-labels," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Id
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Id
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/thin+element"} thin
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+morphism"} identity morphism
            is thin. They depend on lower dimensional features, (notice however that a simplicial set by itself is not equipped with any notion of composition of simplices, nor really, therefore, of identities. See
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasicategory"} quasicategory
            for a kind of simplicial set which does have such notions and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+T-complex"} simplicial T-complex
            for more on the intuitions behind this idea of “thinness”).
          %h2#Examples Examples
          %h3#the_empty_simplicial_set The empty simplicial set
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+simplicial+set"} empty simplicial set
            is a simplicial set.
          %h3#simplices_yoneda_embeddings
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            \-simplices (Yoneda embeddings)
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [n]
            denote the object of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex+category"} simplex category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            corresponding to the totally ordered set
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{ 0, 1, 2,\ldots, n\}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta(-, [n])
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta[n]
            is an example of a simplicial set. In particular we have
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mi m
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi Hom
                    %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta[n]_m=Hom_\Delta([m],[n])
            and hence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta[n]_m
            is a finite set with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mi m
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                      %mi n
                    %mo )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \binom{n+m+1}{n}
            elements.
          %p
            By the Yoneda lemma, the
            = succeed "-simplices" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            are in natural bijective correspondence to maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta[n] \rightarrow X
            of simplicial sets.
          %h3#the_minimal_simplicial_circle The minimal simplicial circle
          %p
            See at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/minimal+simplicial+circle"} minimal simplicial circle
          %h3#cartesian_products_of_simplices Cartesian products of simplices
          %p
            The non-degenerate
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplices"} simplices
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+product+of+simplices"} Cartesian product of simplices
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Delta[1] \times \Delta[2]
          %p
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-simplex"} 1-simplex
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta[1]
            with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-simplex"} 2-simplex
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta[2]
            (i.e. the canonical simplicial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cylinder+object"} cylinder object
            over the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-simplex"} 2-simplex
          %p
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/SimplicialCylinderOn2Simplex.jpg", :width => "240"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              graphics grabbed from
              %a{:href => "#Friedman08"} Friedman 08, p. 33
          %p
            For more on this and related examples see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+of+simplices"} product of simplices
          %h3#simplicial_complexes Simplicial complexes
          %p
            Every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+complex"} simplicial complex
            can be viewed a simplicial set (in several different ways).
          %p
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/ASimplicialComplex.jpg", :width => "180"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              graphics grabbed form
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.06129"} arXiv:1710.06129
          %p
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/AnotherSimplicialComplex.jpg", :width => "400"}/
          %blockquote
            %p graphics grabbed from Maletic, 2013
          %p In particular any graph is thought of as being built of vertices and edges and so is a (1-dimensional) simplicial complex. Choosing a direction on the edges then gives a directed graph and that gives a simplicial set, as follows.
          %h3#directed_graphs Directed graphs
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+graph"} directed graph
            (with loops and multiple edges allowed, i.e., a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quiver"} quiver
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo ⇉
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \rightrightarrows V
            is essentially the same thing as a 1-dimensional simplicial set, by taking
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_0 \coloneqq V
            to be the set of vertices and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi E
                  %mo ⊎
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_1 \coloneqq E \uplus V
            to be the disjoint union of the set of edges with the set of vertices (the latter corresponding to the degenerate 1-simplices).
          %h3#nerve_of_a_category Nerve of a category
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is a small category, the
            %strong nerve
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is a simplicial set which we denote
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi NC
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} NC
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [n]
            defined above as a category, we define the
            = succeed "-simplices" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi NC
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} NC
            to be the set of functors
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [n] \rightarrow C
            = succeed "-simplices" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi NC
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} NC
            are the objects of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            = succeed "-simplices" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            = succeed "-simplices" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            composable arrows in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_0
            and
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_n
          %h3#singular_simplicial_complex_of_a_topological_space Singular simplicial complex of a topological space
          %p
            Recall from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex+category"} simplex category
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+realization"} geometric realization
            the standard functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo →
                  %mi Top
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta \to Top
            which sends
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [n] \in \Delta
            to the standard topological
            = succeed "-simplex" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta^n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            the simplicial set
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ↦
                  %msub
                    %mi Hom
                    %mi Top
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S X : [n] \mapsto Hom_{Top}(\Delta^n, X)
          %p
            called the
            %strong simplicial singular complex
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complex"} Kan complex
            and may be regarded as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} fundamental ∞-groupoid
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            Following up on the idea of ‘’thinness’’, a singular simplex
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: \Delta^n \to X
            may be called
            %strong thin
            if it factors through a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/retraction"} retraction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Λ
                    %mi i
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r: \Delta^n \to \Lambda^{n-1}_i
            to some horn of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta^n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complex"} Kan
            condition on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S X
            can be strengthened to say that every horn in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S X
            has a
            %em thin
            filler. This also helps to give some intuitive underpinning to the idea of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/thin+element"} thin element
            in this simplicial context.
          %h3#bar_construction Bar construction
          %p
            For the moment see
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bar+construction"} bar construction
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#BasicProperties Basic properties
          %p
          #SimplicialSetIsColimitOfItsSimplices.num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
                is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
                of its
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+elements"} elements
              %br/
              Every simplicial set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi sSet
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \in sSet
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
              of its simplices, in that
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mo ≃
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %munder
                              %mi lim
                              %mrow
                                %mi Δ
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                                %mi k
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                                %mo ∈
                                %mi el
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi X
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mi Δ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi k
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mo ≃
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %msup
                              %mo ∫
                              %mrow
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                                %mi k
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                                %mo ∈
                                %mi Δ
                            %mi Δ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi k
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                            %mo ×
                            %msub
                              %mi X
                              %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\begin{aligned}
                    X
                    \&amp;
                    \\;\simeq\;
                    \\underset{
                    \\Delta[k] \in el(X)
                    }{lim}
                    \\Delta[k]
                    \\\
                    \&amp; \;\simeq\;
                    \\int^{[k] \in \Delta} \Delta[k] \times X_k
                    \\end{aligned}
            %p
          Here the first line shows a
          %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
          over the
          %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+elements"} category of elements
          of
          %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
            %semantics
              %mrow
                %mi X
              %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          (e.g.
          = succeed ")" do
            %a{:href => "#GoerssJardine09"} Goerss &amp; Jardine, I, Lemma 2.1
          = succeed "-expression" do
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coend"} coend
          = succeed ")." do
            %a{:href => "end#CoendAsColimitOverCategoryOfElements"} this Prop.
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              Since
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SimplicialSets"} SimplicialSets
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+presheaves"} category of presheaves
              (over the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex+category"} simplex category
              = succeed ")" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Δ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} representables
              (
              %a{:href => "presheaf#EveryPresheafIsColimitOfRepresentables"}> this Prop.
              , the “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/co-Yoneda+lemma"}> co-Yoneda lemma
              ”).
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              In fact, the colimit in Prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SimplicialSetIsColimitOfItsSimplices"}
              is a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+colimit"} homotopy colimit
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "homotopy+limit#SimplicialSetIsHomotopyColimitOverItself"} there
          %h3#classifying_topos Classifying topos
          %h4#simplicial_sets Simplicial sets
          %p
            The category of simplicial sets is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf+category"} presheaf category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topos"} Grothendieck topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+topos"} classifying topos
            of the theory of “intervals”, meaning
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/totally+ordered+sets"} totally ordered sets
            equipped with distinct top and bottom elements.
          %p
            Specifically, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            is a topos containing such an interval
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo →
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta \to E
            sending
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [n]
            to the subobject
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ≤
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi …
                  %mo ≤
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ↪
                  %msup
                    %mi I
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{ (x_1,x_2,\dots,x_n) \;|\; x_1 \le x_2 \le \dots \le x_n \} \hookrightarrow I^n
          %p
            The corresponding
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+realization"} geometric realization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo ⇆
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Δ
                        %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \leftrightarrows Set^{\Delta^{op}}
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphism"} geometric morphism
            which classifies
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta^1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generic+interval"} generic interval
            for more.
          %p
            The usual geometric realization into
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            cannot be obtained in this way precisely, since
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            is not a topos. However, there are
            = succeed "-like" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Johnstone%27s+topological+topos"} Johnstone's topological topos
          %h4#cosimplicial_sets Cosimplicial sets
          %p
            Similarly, also the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^{\Delta}
            of cosimplicial sets is a classifying topos: for inhabited
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+orders"} linear orders
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+topos"} classifying topos
            the section
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/classifying+topos#ForLinearOrders"} For (inhabited) linear orders
          %h3#as_models_in_homotopy_theory As models in homotopy theory
          %p
            (…)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            (…)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complex"} Kan complex
            (…)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-category"} quasi-category
            (…)
          %h3#relation_to_dendroidal_sets Relation to dendroidal sets
          %p
            For the moment see at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dendroidal+set"} dendroidal set
            the section
            %a{:href => "#http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/dendroidal+set#RelationToSimplicialSets"} Relation to simplicial sets
          %h2#variants Variants
          %ul
            %li
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+set"} symmetric set
              is a simplicial set equipped with additional
              %em transposition maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi t
                      %mi i
                      %mi n
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t^n_i: X_n \to X_n
              for
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mi …
                      %mo ,
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i=0,\ldots,n-1
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+group"} symmetric group
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_n
              and satisfy certain commutation relations with the face and degeneracy maps.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+map"} simplicial map
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+object"} simplicial object
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %strong simplicial set
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+simplicial+set"} pointed simplicial set
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+infinity-groupoid"} simplicial infinity-groupoid
                %li
                  %p
